\f0\fs24 \cf0 I'm building a SQL portfolio project to help me get hired for entry-level technical support, customer service, and analyst roles. Project name: "Atlanta Falcons Play-by-Play Analytics Database." I'm a recent Georgia Tech CS grad. I'll be putting this on my resume and LinkedIn and discussing it in interviews, so I need to understand and be able to explain every query and design choice. Please teach as you go. Don't just hand me finished code.\
\
GOAL\
Build a SQLite database from the last 5 NFL seasons (2021-2025) of play-by-play data from the nflverse project, then write 8-10 well-documented SQL queries that analyze team efficiency and play-calling, with a focus on the Atlanta Falcons. Publish it on GitHub (username: msmudano) with a README that shows the questions, the queries, and short plain-English findings.\
\
DATA\
- Source: nflverse play-by-play data (github.com/nflverse/nflverse-data releases). Please check the current file names and format before downloading, and also grab the schedules/games and rosters files if they're useful. If network access is blocked, tell me what to download manually.\
- Tools: Python (pandas) plus the sqlite3 module, or the sqlite3 CLI. Keep the setup simple and reproducible.\
- Keep only the columns I need rather than loading every column. Explain which ones you chose and why.\
\
SKILLS THE QUERIES SHOULD DEMONSTRATE\
Joins across multiple tables, CTEs, window functions (RANK, LAG, rolling averages), CASE WHEN bucketing, GROUP BY with HAVING, and filtering out non-plays (timeouts, kickoffs, etc.) correctly.\
\
QUERIES TO BUILD (adjust if the data suggests better ones)\
1. Success rate by down and distance, Falcons vs. the league\
2. Run vs. pass rate by game situation (score margin, quarter)\
3. Third-down conversion rate by distance bucket\
4. Red-zone efficiency ranked across all 32 teams\
5. Rolling 4-game average of points scored by the Falcons\
6. Top rushers and receivers by EPA per play, with a minimum-plays filter\
7. Home vs. away splits\
Plus 1-3 more of your suggestion.\
\
HOW I WANT TO WORK\
- Work in small steps. For this first session, stop after: project setup, data loaded, a clear schema, and the first 2-3 queries working. Then summarize where we are.\
- Before each query, explain the question and your approach in plain English, then show the SQL with comments.\
- Ask me before making any major design decision (schema layout, what counts as a "play," how to define success rate), and explain the tradeoffs.\
- Validate results with sanity checks (e.g., compare a Falcons total against a known figure) and tell me if anything looks wrong.\
- Never invent numbers. Print and record the real row count and season count, because I'll put them on my resume.\
- Commit to git often with clear messages. Structure the repo with data/ (gitignored if large), sql/ (one file per query), a loader script, and README.md.\
\
README REQUIREMENTS\
Project overview, how to reproduce it, the schema, each question with its query and a 2-3 sentence finding in plain English, and an honest note on how I built it (I used Claude Code as an assistant; I designed the questions and own the analysis).\
